(1) new function for the table: it may provide low voltage access; 
(2) new function for the display and the illumination: there could be photovoltaic elements on the backside of the display and focused lighting in the rooms; 
(3) new function for the keyboard: with piezo elements the keystrokes could be used for supplying power (to a very limited extent); 
(4) new function for network connectors: with additional cores they may be used for lowvoltage power supply; 
(5) new function for the room: under supposition of standardized battery packs there may be battery-pack exchanger units (put a battery pack in, add some money, get a charged battery pack); 
(6) new function for the human and the expansion slot: with a small generator, which fits into the expansion slot, and a crank handle the user could supply the battery pack with energy.

(1) replacement of cable connection with induction: there could be induction loops in the table or in specific mats, which send electrical energy to an induction loop in the notebook computer (sub-principle b); 
(2) replacement of the battery pack with a fuel cell (sub-principle using a wide interpretation); 
(3) replacement of the electrical system with an optical system: there could be photovoltaic elements on the backside of the display and focused lighting or even laser beams in the rooms (sub-principle a).
